<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>

<!--
	Opt-in aspect for the agent security test bed.

	Enable it by adding the line

		secdemo.config.xml

	to "WEB-INF/conf/metaConf.txt" (a commented-out line is already there); disable it by
	commenting the line out again. Nothing in this file is active while the line is commented
	out, so the demo boots unchanged.

	Note when enabling or disabling: withdrawing the "pm" model declaration makes the engine's model
	upgrade delete the module on the next boot, which fails while Project / Ticket instances still
	exist. Start from a fresh database, or delete those instances first. The same applies to the
	"secdemo" module of the previous version of this fixture: a database that ran it holds Note and
	Secret instances of a module this file no longer declares, so it will not boot. See
	doc/security/testbed.md, section "Enabling and resetting the fixture".

	What it wires - the application of doc/security/role-model-worked-example.md, built so that the
	document and the fixture cannot disagree:
	  * the "pm" business model (Project / Ticket),
	  * two roles and two groups - one role vocabulary serving both grant tables,
	  * four declarative role rules on the access manager: two tiers on the security root, one scope
	    derived from Project#partners, and a security parent making a ticket's project its parent,
	  * the <security-config> that the engine's ModelAccessRights evaluates, per object and per
	    attribute,
	  * the <tool-access> policy that tl-ai's tool layer evaluates,
	  * three TL-Script tool factories, the tools of two of them classified in <tool-groups> and the
	    tool of the third deliberately left unclassified,
	  * the component (layout) access every persona needs to reach the agent chat and the
	    TL-Script console,
	  * SecDemoSetupService, which seeds the persona accounts, the example projects and tickets, and
	    verifies at boot that the declarative rules actually produced the roles the matrix claims.

	What it deliberately does NOT wire: "use-default-security-parent". The engine default is false and
	this fixture needs it, because role resolution is downward only - making the security root the
	security parent of everything would put a root-held role on every object, the tier assignment would
	swallow the scope assignment, and per-object scoping could not be demonstrated at all. The previous
	version of this fixture set it to true, which is why it could only ever show tier-based access.

	doc/security/testbed.md is the entry point: it maps every behaviour to a persona, a thing to
	type and an expected result.
-->
<application xmlns:config="http://www.top-logic.com/ns/config/6.0">
	<services>
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.util.model.ModelService">
			<instance>
				<declarations>
					<declaration file="/WEB-INF/model/pm.model.xml"/>
				</declarations>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			Two roles, and both of them appear in BOTH grant tables below. That is the point of the
			whole fixture: one role vocabulary answers two different questions, because the two tables
			ask about the role at different objects.

			  projectPartner  on the security root  = "project partners may use the write tools"  (tier)
			  projectPartner  on project Alpha      = "you are a partner of Alpha"                (scope)

			No separate "agent role" is invented alongside a "model role". Roles must exist before
			SecurityConfigurationService and AiToolAccessService start, which is guaranteed because both
			declare a dependency on InitialRolesManager.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.base.services.InitialRolesManager">
			<instance>
				<roles>
					<role name="projectPartner"/>
					<role name="ticketReader"/>
				</roles>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			The groups whose membership the tier rules read. Membership is what an administrator
			maintains; everything else is derived.

			No "default-group" is declared: the worked example sets one because it builds an
			application from scratch, and this is an aspect over an application that already has its
			own account defaults. Nothing in the fixture depends on a group every account joins.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.base.services.InitialGroupManager">
			<instance>
				<groups>
					<group name="projectPartners"/>
					<group name="ticketReaders"/>
				</groups>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			The heart of the fixture: four declarative rules, contributed additively to whatever access
			manager the application already runs. No "class" is named on purpose - tl-element configures
			StorageAccessManager, which maintains the security storage, and replacing it here would
			change far more than this aspect is allowed to.

			Three mechanisms, three jobs, worth naming individually because people conflate them:

			  <singleton-rule>    puts a role on one well-known object. Used here for the security root,
			                      which is the only place a tier can live, and the only place
			                      <tool-access> can see. Replaces the TL 7 <role-assignment>.
			  <rule type=         derives a role on every matching object by navigating a <path> of
			  "reference">        business attributes to the accounts that should hold it. A path may end
			                      at a Group or at a Person; a Person is resolved to their
			                      representative group automatically.
			  <security-parents>  decides what "ancestor" means for role resolution, and therefore what
			                      a scoped role reaches downward.

			Every rule needs a unique "id" in TL 8 - that is what the ticket #29221 migration adds to
			older configurations.

			On the <path> wrapper: NavigationRuleConfig.getPathElements() is @Name("path") and
			@DefaultContainer, so a step may be written inside <path>...</path> or directly as a child of
			the rule. Both parse. Ticket #29221's description shows the unwrapped form; this file uses
			the wrapper because it makes the two-element case unambiguous, and
			TestRoleRulePathNesting in tl-ai-demo asserts that the two spellings produce the same rule.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tool.boundsec.manager.AccessManager">
			<instance>
				<role-rules>
					<rules>

						<!--
							TIER 1. Members of "projectPartners" hold projectPartner on the security
							root. This, and only this, is what <tool-access> can see.
						-->
						<singleton-rule id="secdemo.tier-projectPartner"
							target="SecurityStructure#ROOT"
							role="projectPartner"
						>
							<path>
								<group-with-name name="projectPartners"/>
							</path>
						</singleton-rule>

						<!-- TIER 2. Same shape for ticket readers. -->
						<singleton-rule id="secdemo.tier-ticketReader"
							target="SecurityStructure#ROOT"
							role="ticketReader"
						>
							<path>
								<group-with-name name="ticketReaders"/>
							</path>
						</singleton-rule>

						<!--
							SCOPE. Whoever is listed in a project's "partners" attribute holds
							projectPartner ON THAT PROJECT. Derived from business data, per object.
							This is the assignment the previous fixture could not express.
						-->
						<rule id="secdemo.scope-projectPartner"
							meta-element="pm:Project"
							role="projectPartner"
							type="reference"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="pm:Project#partners"/>
							</path>
						</rule>

						<!--
							TYPE SCOPE. Members of "ticketReaders" hold ticketReader on EVERY ticket.

							This rule is easy to think unnecessary and is not, and getting it wrong is
							the single most instructive mistake this fixture can teach. The two tier
							rules above put ticketReader on the security root, and <tool-access> is
							satisfied by that - but <security-config> is not. Model access resolves a
							role by walking the object and the object's security ANCESTORS, and with
							use-default-security-parent left at false the security root is not an
							ancestor of anything. So "pm:Ticket grants Read to ticketReader" plus
							"ticketReader on the root" grants nothing at all: Ben would hold the read
							tools and be refused every ticket, and the refusal would look like an empty
							database.

							A role on the root is therefore a statement about a PERSON, not about data.
							Every per-object and per-type guarantee needs the role on the object, which
							for "may read all tickets" means a rule producing it on every instance of
							the type. A <path> that is only a <group-with-name>, with no <step>, is how
							that is written - the same shape a <singleton-rule> uses, applied to a type
							instead of to one well-known object.
						-->
						<rule id="secdemo.type-ticketReader"
							meta-element="pm:Ticket"
							role="ticketReader"
							type="reference"
						>
							<path>
								<group-with-name name="ticketReaders"/>
							</path>
						</rule>

					</rules>
				</role-rules>

				<!--
					A ticket's security parent is the project that contains it, so a partner's scope on
					a project reaches that project's tickets without a second role rule. Navigating the
					composition backwards is what "the project that contains me" means.
				-->
				<security-parents>
					<rules>
						<rule id="secdemo.ticket-in-project"
							meta-element="pm:Ticket"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="pm:Project#tickets"
									inverse="true"
								/>
							</path>
						</rule>
					</rules>
				</security-parents>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			L2, the policy the engine enforces per object and per attribute. Deny-by-default, and it is
			real: a type with no grant for an operation resolves to an empty role set, and a check
			against an empty role set is false. You never write a denial.

			Three properties of this table matter more than its contents:

			  * pm:Ticket has NO Delete grant, for anybody. So nobody deletes a ticket, and no <deny>
			    was needed to achieve it.
			  * The <part> grant on pm:Ticket#body NARROWS the class-level Read: an attribute-level
			    grant is only consulted after the class-level check has passed, and its absence means
			    the attribute inherits that decision. So a ticketReader reads a ticket and its title,
			    and pm:Ticket#body is absent from what any tool reports to it.
			  * Both roles appear here AND in <tool-access>. Neither table knows about the other; what
			    connects them is the role name and nothing else.

			Administrators and engine super-users bypass all of it, and so does any code running in a
			system context. An admin account proves nothing.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.model.security.SecurityConfigurationService">
			<instance>
				<security-config>
					<class name="pm:Project">
						<grant operation="Read"
							roles="projectPartner"
						/>
						<grant operation="Write"
							roles="projectPartner"
						/>
						<grant operation="Create"
							roles="projectPartner"
						/>
					</class>
					<class name="pm:Ticket">
						<grant operation="Read"
							roles="projectPartner, ticketReader"
						/>
						<grant operation="Write"
							roles="projectPartner"
						/>
						<grant operation="Create"
							roles="projectPartner"
						/>
					</class>
					<part name="pm:Ticket#body">
						<grant operation="Read"
							roles="projectPartner"
						/>
					</part>
				</security-config>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			Three TL-Script tool factories, which exist to make two mechanisms testable end to end rather
			than only in a unit test: the <tool-groups> classification, and the boundary of the read
			filtering of issue #289.

			A TL-Script tool has no @ToolMethod annotation to carry a group, so it arrives ungrouped and
			is therefore denied to everyone. The only way to give it one is to name it in a <tool-groups>
			entry - see the block below. That is the migration path every application with script tools
			has to walk.

			"secdemo-reporting.countTickets" and "secdemo-unclassified.countTicketsUnclassified" have
			the same body, the same mode and the same parameters. The single difference between them is
			that the first is named in <tool-groups> and the second is not, so the pair isolates the
			classification mechanism exactly.

			"secdemo-reporting.countVisibleTickets" is the honest counterpart of countTickets and the
			reason both exist: per-object read filtering cannot narrow an aggregate, because a count is
			a number by the time any filter sees it (issue #291). A tool that wants a filtered count has
			to filter inside the script, which is what filterSecurity does. Neither tool lies about
			which one it is.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tl.ai.tool.api.registry.ToolRegistryService">
			<instance>
				<tool-factories>
					<factory name="secdemo-reporting">
						<implementation class="com.top_logic.tl.ai.core.tools.registry.ScriptToolFactory">
							<tools>
								<tool name="countTickets"
									mode="READ"
								>
									<description>
										<en>Counts every ticket, including ones the acting account may not read - an aggregate computed inside a script is not read-filtered. Named in tool-groups, which puts it in data.read.</en>
										<de>Zählt alle Tickets, auch die für das ausführende Konto nicht lesbaren - eine im Skript berechnete Kennzahl wird nicht gefiltert. Ist in tool-groups eingetragen und gehört damit zur Gruppe data.read.</de>
									</description>
									<implementation>all(`pm:Ticket`).size()</implementation>
								</tool>
								<tool name="countVisibleTickets"
									mode="READ"
								>
									<description>
										<en>Counts the tickets the acting account may read, by filtering inside the script before counting. The honest way to report a count.</en>
										<de>Zählt die für das ausführende Konto lesbaren Tickets, indem im Skript vor dem Zählen gefiltert wird. Der ehrliche Weg, eine Anzahl zu melden.</de>
									</description>
									<implementation>filterSecurity(all(`pm:Ticket`)).size()</implementation>
								</tool>
							</tools>
						</implementation>
					</factory>
					<factory name="secdemo-editing">
						<implementation class="com.top_logic.tl.ai.core.tools.registry.ScriptToolFactory">
							<tools>
								<tool name="addTicket"
									mode="DATA_WRITE"
								>
									<description>
										<en>Creates a ticket with the given title in the given project. Named in tool-groups, which puts it in data.write.</en>
										<de>Erzeugt ein Ticket mit dem angegebenen Titel im angegebenen Projekt. Ist in tool-groups eingetragen und gehört damit zur Gruppe data.write.</de>
									</description>
									<parameters>
										<parameter name="project"
											type="pm:Project"
											mandatory="true"
										/>
										<parameter name="title"
											type="tl.core:String"
											mandatory="true"
										/>
									</parameters>
									<implementation>$project.set(`pm:Project#tickets`, $project.get(`pm:Project#tickets`).concat(new(`pm:Ticket`)..set(`pm:Ticket#title`, $title)))</implementation>
								</tool>
							</tools>
						</implementation>
					</factory>
					<factory name="secdemo-unclassified">
						<implementation class="com.top_logic.tl.ai.core.tools.registry.ScriptToolFactory">
							<tools>
								<tool name="countTicketsUnclassified"
									mode="READ"
								>
									<description>
										<en>Counts every ticket, unfiltered. Deliberately left out of tool-groups, so it belongs to no group and is denied to every role.</en>
										<de>Zählt alle Tickets, ungefiltert. Absichtlich nicht in tool-groups eingetragen, gehört daher zu keiner Gruppe und ist für jede Rolle gesperrt.</de>
									</description>
									<implementation>all(`pm:Ticket`).size()</implementation>
								</tool>
							</tools>
						</implementation>
					</factory>
				</tool-factories>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			L1, the tl-ai tool layer: which roles may execute which agent tools. Independent of the
			<security-config> above, which governs the data.

			"data.write" is a tier and cannot be anything else. At the moment this gate runs, the model
			has proposed a tool call and there is no object yet to check, so this table answers exactly
			one question: may the agent wield this kind of power on your behalf at all. Every question
			of the form "on which object" belongs to <security-config> and is answered later, per
			object. That is not a limitation to work around; it is the reason this table reads roles on
			the security root, because the root is where a deployment-wide statement about a person
			lives.

			The consequence the personas are built to show: Anna holds data.write globally and still
			cannot write project Beta. L1 was never going to be what stopped her, and it did not have to
			be.

			"script" is granted to both roles because the scenarios that exercise the MODEL rights ask
			the agent to use the TL-Script tool, and denying it at the tool layer would stop them before
			the layer they are about. Committing with it costs data.write on top, which ticketReader
			does not hold - so "a partner creates a ticket / a reader cannot" is a policy difference
			rather than a model-rights accident.

			Nothing is reached implicitly: every group is named. "model.structure", "egress",
			"workspace", "agent.authoring" and "agent.meta" are granted to nobody, so the model editor,
			the web fetch, the task tools and the authoring tools are denied to every persona -
			deny-by-default, no <deny> needed.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tl.ai.core.security.AiToolAccessService">
			<instance class="com.top_logic.tl.ai.core.security.AiToolAccessService">
				<!--
					A TL-Script tool declares no group, so this is the only place it can get one. Every
					tool is named, one at a time: there is no pattern syntax, and a tool that appears
					later - one a createScriptTool call adds to a factory, one a connected MCP server
					grows - is denied until an administrator names it here. That is deliberate, and it
					is what keeps the capability a tool costs a decision the administrator made rather
					than one whoever created the tool made.

					"secdemo-unclassified.countTicketsUnclassified" is deliberately absent: an
					unclassified tool must stay denied to everyone, and the pair of otherwise identical
					tools is what proves this one line is what makes the difference.
				-->
				<tool-groups>
					<group name="data.read"
						tools="secdemo-reporting.countTickets, secdemo-reporting.countVisibleTickets"
					/>
					<group name="data.write"
						tools="secdemo-editing.addTicket"
					/>
				</tool-groups>

				<tool-access>
					<role name="projectPartner">
						<grants>
							<grant group="model.read"/>
							<grant group="data.read"/>
							<grant group="data.write"/>
							<grant group="script"/>
						</grants>
					</role>
					<role name="ticketReader">
						<grants>
							<grant group="model.read"/>
							<grant group="data.read"/>
							<grant group="script"/>
						</grants>
					</role>
				</tool-access>

				<!--
					An explicitly *empty* baseline, which is not the same as leaving the element out.

					Omitting it means "give every user the read-only default" - model.read and data.read
					(#293). This fixture needs the opposite: the matrix says what each role reaches, and
					handing both read capabilities to every account would make the "refused at L1" rows
					unobservable - starting with Ben, whose ticket creation must be refused at L1
					because he holds no data.write.

					So the element is written and left empty, which says out loud that nothing is
					granted to everyone here. "interaction" - asking a question, loading a skill,
					delegating - is granted implicitly to every role and every run anyway.
				-->
				<baseline/>

				<!--
					The ceiling for a run with nobody to answer an approval prompt - aiAgentChatAsync,
					which the TL-Script harness uses, and the MCP endpoint.

					"script" is on the list only because that harness needs it. "data.write" is
					deliberately off it, so projectPartner reaches "secdemo-editing.addTicket" in the
					chat and is refused it in a background run. That contrast is the whole point of the
					mechanism.

					Note what "script" on this list does *not* buy: executing TL-Script with
					withCommit=true additionally costs "data.write", which is off the list, so an
					unattended run cannot persist through the script tool either - not even as an
					administrator, since the allowlist binds them too.
				-->
				<background-allowlist>
					<grant group="model.read"/>
					<grant group="data.read"/>
					<grant group="script"/>
				</background-allowlist>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			The third, independent security layer: component (layout) access. The policies above decide
			what a role may do with the data and which tools it may call, this decides which views a
			role may open at all. All three are needed - a role with model rights and tool grants but no
			view rights logs in to an application without a single tab.

			The format is the engine's own "WEB-INF/conf/security.xml" format
			(com.top_logic.tool.boundsec.config.AccessConfiguration). It is applied here instead of in
			that file because the engine's import resets the access of every view in the application
			before applying it, which an opt-in aspect must not do. The entries below are added, nothing
			else is touched.

			View names are the names of the enclosing "securityLayout" components; only those can hold
			access rights. "Read" is the command group that decides visibility. Nesting does not
			inherit, so every securityLayout on the way to a view needs its own grant. The script
			console sits three levels deep (the tabs labelled "Administration", "Development" and
			"Script console"), hence three entries; the sibling views on those levels stay closed
			because they are security components of their own with no grant.

			The profile is named "*", which SecDemoSetupService expands to every role a configured
			persona is expected to hold. Every persona therefore sees exactly the same UI, which is what
			makes the allow/deny pairs comparable: the only thing that differs between two personas is
			the policy, never what they can reach.

			Granting the script console to a non-administrator role lets that role execute arbitrary
			TL-Script. That is deliberate here and confined to this opt-in aspect: the walkthrough
			asserts that the model access rights hold even for a user who can run any expression. Only
			"Read" is granted, which covers opening the view and its "execute" button; the "execute and
			save changes" button is bound to "Write" and stays disabled - the agent runs started from
			the console do their own writes in their own transaction on the background thread.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.ai.demo.secdemo.SecDemoSetupService">
			<instance class="com.top_logic.ai.demo.secdemo.SecDemoSetupService">
				<!--
					The persona matrix. One row per account: which groups it joins, which role the tier
					rule is therefore expected to give it on the security root, which projects it
					partners on, and what the persona is here to demonstrate.

					The roles are NOT assigned here - the declarative rules above do that, and that is
					the whole difference from the previous version of this fixture. They are stated so
					that the boot log is a readable index of the fixture, and so that the service can
					*verify* the rules produced them. A mismatch is a warning naming the persona and the
					role, which is exactly the failure a hand-assigned role used to hide.
				-->
				<personas>
					<persona login="anna"
						groups="projectPartners"
						roles="projectPartner"
						partner-of="Alpha"
						purpose="tier and scope: write tools globally, and only project Alpha to write them on"
					/>
					<persona login="ben"
						groups="ticketReaders"
						roles="ticketReader"
						purpose="reads every ticket, writes none, and is refused a ticket creation at L1 - the loud case"
					/>
					<persona login="cara"
						groups="projectPartners, ticketReaders"
						roles="projectPartner, ticketReader"
						partner-of="Beta"
						purpose="both roles: partner of Beta, reader of every ticket - reads a ticket of Alpha and cannot edit it"
					/>
					<persona login="dora"
						groups="projectPartners"
						roles="projectPartner"
						purpose="the failure mode: holds the tier, scopes nothing. Write tools and nothing to write, and reads come back empty rather than denied"
					/>
				</personas>

				<!--
					The example projects. Each one names the partners it grants the scope role to, by
					login, and the tickets it contains. Anna partners on Alpha, Cara on Beta, and Dora
					on nothing - which is what makes her the failure mode rather than a fourth partner.
				-->
				<projects>
					<project name="Alpha"
						partners="anna"
					>
						<tickets>
							<ticket title="Alpha: cut the release branch"
								body="Tag the build, then update the changelog."
							/>
							<ticket title="Alpha: fix the login timeout"
								body="Sessions expire after 30s instead of 30min."
							/>
						</tickets>
					</project>
					<project name="Beta"
						partners="cara"
					>
						<tickets>
							<ticket title="Beta: import the customer list"
								body="The CSV export of the old system has a BOM."
							/>
						</tickets>
					</project>
				</projects>

				<component-access>
					<profile name="*">
						<!-- The "Albert" tab. -->
						<view name="tl-chat/chat/tabLayout.layout.xml#TabLayout">
							<commandGroup name="Read"/>
						</view>
						<!-- The chat itself, a security component of its own. -->
						<view name="tl-chat/chat/agent.layout.xml#agent">
							<commandGroup name="Read"/>
						</view>
						<!-- The three nested tabs leading to the script console. -->
						<view name="admin/index.layout.xml#adminView">
							<commandGroup name="Read"/>
						</view>
						<view name="admin/studio/index.layout.xml#studioView">
							<commandGroup name="Read"/>
						</view>
						<view name="com.top_logic.model.search/admin/scriptConsole.layout.xml#view">
							<commandGroup name="Read"/>
						</view>
					</profile>
				</component-access>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<config service-class="com.top_logic.basic.module.ModuleSystem">
			<instance>
				<modules>
					<!-- Seeds the persona accounts, their group memberships and the example data. -->
					<module key="com.top_logic.ai.demo.secdemo.SecDemoSetupService$Module"
						value="true"
					/>
				</modules>
			</instance>
		</config>
	</services>
</application>
